#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# q_factorial

#% text
# `q_factorial(n, q)` gives the q-factorial `[n]_q!`, `(q; q)_n/(1 - q)^n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The q-factorial is the product of the q-numbers `(1 - q^k)/(1 - q) = 1 + q + ... + q^(k - 1)` for `k` from 1 to `n`; at `q = 1` it is `factorial(n)`.

#% item
# The count comes first, as in `factorial(n)`: `q_factorial(n, q)` (it was `q_factorial(q, n)` before round 19).

#% item
# With a symbolic `q` and an integer `n` the result is a polynomial; machine numbers give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
q_factorial(3, q)
#% output
#= (1 + q)*(1 + q + q^2)

#% cell
q_factorial(4, 2)
#% output
#= 315

#% cell
q_factorial(5, 0.5)
#% output
#= 9.5361328125

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The limit `q -> 1` is the factorial:

#% cell
q_factorial(5, 1)
#% output
#= 120

#% cell
q_factorial(5, 0.99999)
#% output
#= 119.99400014499783

